# The program hands the closure of `target` to nobody and lays nothing on it, so linked() takes its closures to stay as they are made, and compiled code takes `call` and `apply` off Function.prototype without a look.
# `poke` is a free name that each run defines, and it is handed nothing. It takes the keeper out of the heap (aotFindObjectOfLayout()), a road that no program has, and reads the closure off it: each run does one deed that is ruled out, and the run time ends the process at the deed.
# The run time looks at the closure behind the deed. What leaves every lookup as it was goes through: the names are listed, `name` is defined again, `call` is laid with the very function that stands above, the object above is set to what it is, and a store that lays nothing.
# The same deeds where the program hands the closure over stop nothing: deeds-on-a-closure-that-unseen-code-was-handed-go-through.ir.
# OPTIONS: mechanisms=all
# RUN: run-in-shell globalThis.poke = () => { const run = aotFindObjectOfLayout(0).run; print(run.name, run.length, Object.keys(run).length); Object.defineProperty(run, "name", { value: "other" }); print(run.name); run.call = Function.prototype.call; Object.setPrototypeOf(run, Function.prototype); Object.preventExtensions(run); run.late = 1; print(run.late); };
# CHECK: base 1 base 2 base 3
# CHECK-NEXT: target 1 0
# CHECK-NEXT: other
# CHECK-NEXT: undefined
# CHECK-NEXT: base 1 base 2 base 3
# CHECK-NEXT: exited with 0
